To change the CSS of a component in our codebase, please use Emotion styled. By convention, we prefix styling props with a dollar to distinguish them from attributes:

```ts
import { styled } from 'storybook/theming'

const ZoomResetButton = styled(ActionList.Button)<{ $isInitialValue }>(
  ({ $isInitialValue }) => ({
    visibility: $isInitialValue ? 'hidden' : undefined
  })
);
```

And you can then use that button on line 84, by passing it the relevant props:

```ts
<ZoomResetButton
  size="small"
  padding="small"
  hidden={value === INITIAL_ZOOM_LEVEL}
  $isInitialValue={value === INITIAL_ZOOM_LEVEL}
  onClick={() => zoomTo(INITIAL_ZOOM_LEVEL)}
  ariaLabel="Reset zoom"
>
```

In particular, CodeRabbit shouted at you because you used a prop name (`hidden`) that matches a HTML attribute. This can lead to situations where the attribute is applied in the DOM even if you didn't want it to, so we prefer to keep names distinct.
